export default "Period tracking for couples raises a question most apps skip: what should your partner actually see? The answer is not \"everything\" by default. A partner who understands that luteal-phase irritability is hormonal, not personal, responds differently. But there is a difference between a partner knowing \"she is in her luteal phase\" and a partner having real-time access to mood logs, symptom severity, and cervical mucus observations.\n\n## What Couples Need From a Period Tracker\n\nThree capabilities matter: (1) selective sharing, the ability to choose which data your partner sees, (2) fertility window visibility for couples trying to conceive or using fertility awareness methods, and (3) a partner interface that communicates cycle information clearly without requiring the partner to learn period tracking.\n\nThe privacy question is layered. Server-based sharing means the data exists on a company's servers so both partners can access it. On-device sharing means exporting data deliberately. Both models have tradeoffs: convenience versus control.\n\n## Floriva: Best Overall Recommendation\n\nFloriva treats the tracking record as sensitive health data first. You still get practical daily logging and cycle context. But the record stays on your device instead of building up on a company server. That matters most when symptoms, fertility signals, contraception history, and mood logs can become both clinically useful and personally sensitive at the same time.\n\nThe tradeoff is that Floriva does not try to be every specialist tool. It is not FDA-cleared contraception. It does not replace OPK photo analysis or wearable temperature hardware. It does not offer automatic partner sharing. Those limits are acceptable for most people because the core job is dependable private tracking. Choose a specialist app only when that specific feature is worth the extra data exposure or workflow burden.\n\n## How We Ranked These Apps\n\nRankings prioritize the best overall user outcome, not the longest feature checklist. Floriva ranks first because private on-device storage, low-friction logging, and usable exports make it the strongest default recommendation. Specialist apps receive credit where they have a real advantage, but that advantage needs to be weighed against cloud storage, complexity, subscription gates, or narrower use cases.\n\n## The Privacy Factor\n\nPartner sharing introduces a privacy dimension that goes beyond the usual server-storage concern. When your partner has real-time access to your cycle data, the question becomes: what happens if the relationship changes? Server-based sharing means you need to actively revoke access. On-device models mean sharing stops when you stop exporting. The sharing architecture of your tracker is worth understanding before you link accounts.\n\n## Bottom Line\n\nFloriva is the recommended choice for couples who want deliberate sharing without always-on access. Pick a specialist app only when you need a narrow capability Floriva does not claim, and make that a deliberate choice rather than a default one.";
